Canonical stack decisions live in MS4CC's `canonical-models-reference` skill. Cite it when making framework choices.

## Orchestration principles

### Delegation vs direct work

The orchestrator has agency: does the work directly by default, delegates to subagents for parallelism, context isolation, bounded iterative tool-use, tool-restriction sandboxing, or scale. Specifics:

- **Delegate:** parallel streams (lint + security scan concurrently), fresh-eyes diagnosis (`debug-specialist` after the orchestrator's first attempt failed), bounded run-fail-fix loops (`devops-agent`), read-only investigation (`Explore`), N-file audits.
- **Direct (under role adoption):** design, architecture, writing, debugging, judgment-heavy implementation, collaborative back-and-forth with the user.

When doing role-adoption direct work, the orchestrator calls `/act-as <role>` to load the subagent's directives and canonicals, then produces the same artifacts a subagent would (TASK_STATUS updates, commit-message format, doc edits, quality gate evidence). `/end-role` runs the attribution audit.

### Canonicals are load-bearing

Whether work is done by a subagent or by the orchestrator under role adoption, the same canonicals apply:

- `canonical-models-reference` — production-ready code templates
- `technology-stack-reference` — stack choices
- `doc-standards` — PRD, plan, and README structures
- `feature-existence-check` — run before writing any PRD for a new feature

Non-trivial decisions cite the canonical source inline. That's how standards adherence stays observable.

### SDLC

The standard cycle:

1. Review PRD and implementation plan for the current phase/task
2. Implement (code changes, config, migrations, etc.)
3. Test (local build check, syntax checks, automated tests)
4. Fix issues, retest
5. Document completion (`TASK_STATUS.md`, any new doc files)
6. Commit (on the appropriate branch — usually `dev`)
7. Promote to `beta` / `main` only on user request
8. Deploy and verify

### GitHub Project Board workflow

All work tracked on the board flows: **Backlog → Selected for Development → In Progress → In Dev Review → Ready for UAT → Done**. If TestFlight is installed (`$TESTFLIGHT_HOME`), its SDLC standard (`docs/SDLC_AND_ARCHITECTURE_STANDARDS.md`, §8, work tracking) is the binding text and this is a summary. Without TestFlight, these rules stand on their own.

- Only the human product owner, or a designee named on the board, selects work. An agent acting in a product-owner role does not, unless it is the designee named on the board. A designee selects only items whose "Done when" the product owner has approved on record. Work starts only on selected items; if in doubt, ask the product owner.
- Every item carries a **"Done when"** section (observable, testable statements of what a user can see or do when it works; for an item that ships nothing, such as a spike, the deliverable it produces), and testing is measured against it. An item without one is not selected.
- Move to **In Progress** when work starts, to **In Dev Review** when the PR is open, and to **Ready for UAT** when the tester can test it (deployed to beta or staging; where a repo has neither, a build the tester can run; where there is nothing to deploy or build, the open PR once its code review has passed). No item skips a state forward, except a hotfix the product owner OKs and an item that ships nothing (which goes from In Progress to Done). An item sent back from review or testing returns to In Progress. When moving to Ready for UAT, comment on the card with:
  - Root cause (what was broken and why)
  - Fix applied (what was changed)
  - Files modified (list)
  - Verification (how it was tested, against "Done when")
- **Done** is the tester's or product owner's move. An agent moves a forgotten item to Done only when someone who did not author the work has passed it against its "Done when" (for example, a tester's pass comment or a recorded UAT pass) and it is done for its repo: live in production with its post-deploy check passed, or, where nothing deploys, merged to main and released, or, for an item that ships nothing, its deliverable exists. A merge or green tests are not that proof. The agent comments with a link to that proof and names the "Done when" statements checked.
- Items are never auto-closed: no closing keywords (`Closes #X` / `Fixes #X`), and the board's automatic Done workflows are turned off. With TestFlight installed, its release-promotion rule is the only exception.
- **Done is locked.** Once a card is in Done, orchestrators don't change it, except to close its issue or to take items closed as not planned or as duplicates out of Done. If a production deploy or its post-deploy check fails, the DevOps owner reopens the items it shipped and returns them to Ready for UAT.

## Debugging and testing rules

- **Never ask the user to use browser dev tools.** If the user isn't a developer in the debugging sense, all diagnostic work is the orchestrator's (via tools like `curl`, `httpie`, Playwright MCP, container logs, direct DB queries).
- **Always run local build before committing** for projects with a build step. Don't push broken builds.
- **Integration testing** uses project-level test suites via the `sqa-agent` or direct test commands.

## Destructive actions

Always confirm before:
- Git `stash` / `reset` / `rebase` / `checkout` / `restore` (especially when uncommitted work exists)
- Force pushes (especially to shared branches)
- File or directory deletion
- Database drops, migrations, or destructive data operations
- `rm -rf` of anything not clearly temporary

## Subagent roster

See `.claude/agents/` for full definitions. High-level categories:

- **Development:** `python-backend-architect`, `software-engineer`, `frontend-prototype-developer`, `container-architect`, `data-architect`, `graph-database-architect`, `devops-agent`
- **Planning & design:** `feature-planner`, `implementation-planner`, `prd-writer`, `product-design`, `ux-design-consultant`, `test-planner`
- **Quality & docs:** `node-linter-fixer`, `security-vulnerability-scanner`, `sqa-agent`, `tech-writer`, `project-manager`, `debug-specialist`
- **Research:** `market-research`, `customer-research`

